        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Among them was one who would strangle a bear and kill it — he would choke it like a chicken on the spot&#x27; — WARRIOR-OF-MARTYRDOM motif distinctive to the Meqabyan trilogy. The brothers are physically formidable — bear-strangling + lion-killing-with-a-stone (2:9) + sword-felling-a-man-with-one-stroke (2:10) — feats reminiscent of SAMSON (Judges 14:5-6 lion-slaying) and DAVID&#x27;S MIGHTY MEN (2 Samuel 23:8-23). The composition stages a MARTYRDOM-OF-WARRIORS: men who COULD fight successfully but CHOOSE surrender to a higher commandment. Distinguishes Meqabyan from the standard LXX 2 Maccabees 7 passive-sufferer martyrology — the brothers&#x27; eventual surrender (2:27 + 3:1ff.) frames martyrdom as a freely-chosen offering of victory-capacity to God, not as a fight lost.</p></aside>',
