        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>&#x27;They were warriors of great strength; and they had comeliness, and the loveliness of speech. Moreover, the beauty of their outward appearance was wonderful; but because they worshiped God and did not fear death, the BEAUTY OF THEIR INWARD HEART SURPASSED ALL&#x27; (vv. 11-12). Contrastive-ethics anchor: outward-beauty + warrior-strength + eloquence are real-but-secondary virtues; the beauty-of-the-inward-heart (worship + fearlessness-of-death) is the SURPASSING VIRTUE. Parallels 1 Peter 3:3-4 (Petrine &#x27;the hidden man of the heart, in that which is not corruptible, even the ornament of a meek and quiet spirit&#x27; — patristically read by Augustine + Chrysostom as the canonical Christian inward-beauty anchor) + 1 Samuel 16:7 (&#x27;man looketh on the outward appearance, but the LORD looketh on the heart&#x27;). The verse frames the brothers&#x27; subsequent martyrdom as the manifestation of inner-virtue under outward-suffering.</p></aside>',
