        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>&#x27;While You are the Creator who saves and who kills, he makes himself as though [the idols] had created him; HE IS ONE WHO SHEDS THE BLOOD OF MAN AND EATS THE FLESH OF MAN&#x27; — child-sacrifice intensifier. The phrase &#x27;sheds the blood of man and eats the flesh of man&#x27; picks up the child-sacrifice motif from 1:14 (priests of Ṣiruṣaydan ate from defiled sacrifices) and intensifies it. The accusation is now placed in the mouth of the martyrs themselves as part of their GROUNDS FOR REFUSAL. Parallels the Hebrew-Bible polemic against Moloch / Molech child-sacrifice (Lev 18:21 + Lev 20:2-5 + 2 Kings 16:3 + 2 Kings 21:6 + Jeremiah 7:31 + Jeremiah 19:5 + Ezekiel 16:20-21) and the Wisdom of Solomon 14:23 polemic (&#x27;they slaughtered the children&#x27;). The Meqabyan placement in the martyrs&#x27; prayer locates the brothers&#x27; resistance theologically.</p></aside>',
