        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>Two younger-brothers appear (3:28-30): &#x27;Come, let us flee, lest these criminals and waverers find us.&#x27; The martyrs refuse to flee; the two younger brothers join them — making FIVE BROTHERS IN TOTAL. **The &#x27;five sons of Maqabis&#x27; formulation is DISTINCTIVE to the Ethiopian narrative** and has NO parallel in the LXX 2 Maccabees account of the seven brothers. The expansion from three-named (Abya/Sila/Fentos) to five-total restructures the Maccabean-martyr-family typology around the Meqabyan-specific five-fold number. Per scholarly apparatus §6 (&#x27;The Maqabean Family of 1 Meqabyan&#x27;): the five-brothers-of-Maqabis-of-Benjamin in 1 Mq is structurally MIRRORED by the five-sons-of-Maqabis-of-Moab in 2 Mq 13:1 (a NUMBER-SYMMETRY across the two books that supports Horovitz&#x27;s structural distinction between 1 Mq and 2 Mq).</p></aside>',
