        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>Heavenly-palace ekphrasis named-dwelling: ABRAHAM + ISAAC + JACOB + DAVID + SOLOMON + HEZEKIAH dwelling-in-their-heavenly-abode. The specific royal roster expands the standard patriarchal-triad (Abraham + Isaac + Jacob — cf. seed 2:22 + 3:38) by adding the Davidic-Solomonic-Hezekian royal-faithfulness exemplars. The addition of HEZEKIAH (rather than the more-expected Josiah or another reform-king) is theologically significant: Hezekiah is the king who CRIED OUT TO GOD UNDER THREAT (2 Kings 19 / Isaiah 37 — Sennacherib&#x27;s threat to Jerusalem; Hezekiah&#x27;s prayer in the Temple) + RECEIVED MIRACULOUS-DELIVERANCE-AND-EXTENDED-LIFE (2 Kings 20 / Isaiah 38 — the sundial-turned-back-ten-degrees). Hezekiah&#x27;s typology fits the Meqabyan brothers&#x27; situation exactly: facing imperial-threat, praying to God, receiving divine-rescue. The chapter&#x27;s heavenly-dwelling vision functions as the brothers&#x27; destination-promise.</p></aside>',
