        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And the WIND WHICH GOD CREATED gives fruit in their midst. So also is the resurrection of the dead&#x27; — closing the four-elements resurrection-parable with the wind-elemental as completion. The argument-from-tree-fruiting is paralleled in JOB 14:7-9 (&#x27;there is hope of a tree, if it be cut down, that it will sprout again, and that the tender branch thereof will not cease... at the scent of water it will bud&#x27;) + ECCLESIASTES 11:5-6 (the wind-and-growing-bones mystery — &#x27;thou knowest not what is the way of the spirit, nor how the bones do grow in the womb of her that is with child&#x27;). Meqabyan unifies the elements-and-fruit pattern into a single direct-analogy: God-makes-the-tree-fruit, so God-raises-the-dead. The chapter&#x27;s later seed-buried argument (8:22) is the COMPLETION of this elemental-analogy structure.</p></aside>',
