        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>Argument-from-ancestral-burial for resurrection: the patriarchs — Adam + Abel + Seth + Noah + Shem + Abraham + Isaac + Jacob + Joseph + Moses + Aaron — sought to be buried with their kin PRECISELY SO THAT, at the resurrection, they would rise together. The reading reverses the typical ancestor-veneration motif: rather than the dead remaining in tombs as a memorial, burial-with-kin becomes a PROVISIONAL-PRACTICE awaiting communal resurrection. Connects to Genesis 23 (Abraham purchasing the Cave of Machpelah for Sarah&#x27;s burial) + Genesis 49:29-32 (Jacob&#x27;s burial instructions) + Joshua 24:32 (Joseph&#x27;s bones brought up from Egypt). The patristic parallel is in resurrection-of-the-flesh treatments — Tertullian De Resurrectione Carnis §52, Theophilus of Antioch Ad Autolycum 1.13.</p></aside>',
