        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>PATRIARCHS-BURIAL CATALOG: Adam + Abel + Seth + Noah + Shem + Abraham + Isaac + Jacob + Joseph + Moses + Aaron — sought to be buried with their kin PRECISELY SO THAT, at the resurrection, they would rise together. The eleven-patriarch sequence is DISTINCTIVE: standard biblical-patriarchal triads are 3 (Abraham/Isaac/Jacob) or 4 (+ Joseph); Meqabyan&#x27;s eleven extends BACKWARDS to Adam (the protoplast) + Abel (the first-righteous-martyr) + Seth (the righteous-replacement) and FORWARDS to Joseph + Moses + Aaron (the Egypt-deliverance leaders). The reading reverses the typical ancestor-veneration motif: burial-with-kin is PROVISIONAL-PRACTICE awaiting communal-resurrection, not memorial-remembrance. Connects to Genesis 23 (Abraham purchasing Cave of Machpelah) + Genesis 49:29-32 (Jacob&#x27;s burial instructions) + Joshua 24:32 (Joseph&#x27;s bones brought up from Egypt) — the ANCESTRAL-BURIAL-FOR-RESURRECTION typology Tewahedo theology preserves at every funeral-liturgy.</p></aside>',
