        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>&#x27;CAPERNAUM, SAMARIA AND GALILEE, SYRIA AND DAMASCUS, CYPRUS AND ACHAIA, and all the heathen of the district of the Jordan&#x27; — NT-ERA APOCALYPTIC TOPONYM CATALOG. The geographic-list is the STRONGEST internal-dating-evidence in 1 Mq for a Christian-era stratum: Capernaum (Mt 11:23 woe-against-Capernaum + the Synoptic Capernaum-Galilean-ministry locus) + Galilee (the standard NT-Galilean-ministry region) + Damascus (Acts 9 Damascus-road Saul-Paul) + Cyprus (Acts 13 Paul-Barnabas mission) + Achaia (Acts 18-19 Pauline-Achaian-mission, including Athens-and-Corinth). The Capernaum-and-Achaia juxtaposition is impossible in pre-Christian-era composition (Capernaum-as-named-significant-locale is a Synoptic-Gospels-era datum; Achaia-as-Roman-province is post-146-BCE but the apocalyptic-pairing-with-Capernaum is NT-era). Per CROSS_REFERENCE_APPENDIX §61 widened-dating: this verse supports the Aksumite-to-Solomonic (4th-14th c.) composition window for the chapter, with the underlying stratum reaching back further.</p></aside>',
