        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>OPENS Ch 23 — HOMILETIC-GENRE ANCHOR: wisdom-and-counsel theme. The chapter likely develops wisdom-and-counsel as Tewahedo-political-theology virtue. The wisdom-and-counsel-of-the-king theme parallels: PROVERBS 8:14-16 (&#x27;Counsel is mine, and sound wisdom: I am understanding; I have strength. By me kings reign, and princes decree justice&#x27;); 1 KINGS 3:5-15 (Solomon&#x27;s wisdom-prayer at Gibeon); 1 KINGS 4:29-34 (Solomon&#x27;s wisdom-greater-than-all-the-children-of-the-east); 2 CHRONICLES 1:7-12 (the parallel); JOB 12:13-16 (with God is wisdom and strength); WISDOM OF SOLOMON 6-9 (the foundational royal-wisdom treatise); SIRACH 10:1-3 (&#x27;a wise judge will instruct his people&#x27;); ISAIAH 11:2 (Spirit-of-counsel + Spirit-of-might). Tewahedo political-theology preserves wisdom-and-counsel as one of the FOUR-CARDINAL-ROYAL-VIRTUES (paralleling Tewahedo-canonical Wisdom-of-Solomon canonicity). The Meqabyan chapter likely articulates wisdom-and-counsel grounded in the post-martyrdom moral-theology.</p></aside>',
