        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>NEBUCHADNEZZAR-TO-DANIEL citation: &#x27;the SPIRIT OF GOD UPON YOU&#x27; — direct echo of DANIEL 5:14 (Belshazzar to Daniel: &#x27;I have even heard of thee, that the spirit of the gods is in thee, and that light and understanding and excellent wisdom is found in thee&#x27;) + DANIEL 4:8-9 (Nebuchadnezzar to Daniel: &#x27;in whom is the spirit of the holy gods... thou canst; for the spirit of the holy gods is in thee&#x27;). The Danielic spirit-of-God-upon-the-prophet vocabulary locates Meqabyan within the DANIELIC PROPHETIC-INSPIRATION tradition. The chapter&#x27;s broader four-kingdoms apocalypse (seed 34:1) extends the Danielic apocalyptic-framework INTO THE ETHIOPIAN HORIZON via the chapter&#x27;s eastward-toponym extensions. The Spirit-upon-the-prophet vocabulary is itself a CHRISTOLOGICAL-PNEUMATOLOGICAL anchor: Luke 4:18 (Christ at Nazareth: &#x27;the Spirit of the Lord is upon me&#x27;) applies Isaiah 61:1&#x27;s same vocabulary to himself. Tewahedo Christology preserves the Spirit-upon-the-prophet pattern as prefiguring Christ&#x27;s-anointing.</p></aside>',
