        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>Final-capstone-doxology completion (paired with seed 36:45 DOCTRINAL-CAPSTONE resurrection-tricolon + DOUBLE-AMEN closing): the verse develops the chapter&#x27;s-closing-coda — the post-tricolon doxological-praise that the chapter closes with. The post-tricolon-praise pattern parallels: PSALM 41:13 (&#x27;Blessed be the LORD God of Israel from everlasting, and to everlasting. Amen, and Amen&#x27; — the Book-One-of-the-Psalter closing); PSALM 72:18-19 + 89:52 (the parallel Psalter-book-closings — Book-II + Book-III); PSALM 106:48 (Book-IV); ROMANS 16:25-27 (the Pauline-final-doxology); HEBREWS 13:20-21 (the benediction-doxology closing Hebrews); JUDE 24-25 (the most-substantive NT doxological-closing); REVELATION 1:5-6 + 5:13 + 7:12 (the multiple-doxological-anchors throughout Revelation). The Meqabyan trilogy&#x27;s BOOK-CLOSING SIGNATURE — 1 Mq 36:45 &#x27;with the double-Amen&#x27; + 2 Mq 21:10 &#x27;DOUBLE-AMEN አሜን፥ አሜን&#x27; + 3 Mq 10:29 &#x27;From today and unto eternity, Amen&#x27; — is anchored in the Psalter&#x27;s three-of-five book-ending-doxologies architectural-pattern. The CHARACTERISTIC MEQABYAN BOOK-CLOSING SIGNATURE is one of the trilogy&#x27;s STRONGEST STRUCTURAL-COHERENCE EVIDENCES per Horovitz 1905&#x27;s internal-redactional-unity argument.</p></aside>',
